1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
MAN: How are you getting on with the assignment on handwriting?

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:05,680
WOMAN: Not too bad. You know

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:11,200
I hadn't realised that children benefit in so many ways from learning to write.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:13,580
WOMAN: It's such an important skill and

5
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:18,393
yet most people think handwriting is less important than in the past

6
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:21,630
because people hardly ever write by hand these days.

7
00:00:21,630 --> 00:00:28,229
MAN: Yes. And all the evidence suggests children should learn to write by hand before they learn to type.

8
00:00:28,229 --> 00:00:30,660
Not least because it helps their memory.

9
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:36,284
WOMAN: That's right. The physical act of writing helps children to remember letters.

10
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:39,900
That seems pretty obvious when you think about it.

11
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:43,904
MAN: What's less obvious is how it helps develop their concentration.

12
00:00:43,904 --> 00:00:47,240
They have to sit still and focus on one thing.

13
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:51,640
WOMAN: Yeah. That aspect of handwriting had never occurred to me before.

14
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:57,780
MAN: Same here. I'm not sure I understand how it improves children's imagination though.

15
00:00:57,780 --> 00:00:59,891
WOMAN: Well, there was that study

16
00:00:59,891 --> 00:01:04,080
which showed that primary aged children generated more ideas

17
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:06,574
when they were writing by hand than using a keyboard.

18
00:01:06,574 --> 00:01:09,740
I would've guessed that would be the case.

19
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:14,652
MAN: Mmm, yeah. I'd never associated spatial awareness with handwriting either.

20
00:01:14,652 --> 00:01:19,500
I thought spatial awareness was more to do with knowing

21
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,840
where you are in relation to objects or other people.

22
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:25,060
WOMAN: I thought that too.

23
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:28,360
But good spatial awareness is essential for writing

24
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:31,720
because you have to space words correctly.

25
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:36,870
WOMAN: It's not just fine motor skills that improve through writing as I'd always assumed.

26
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:41,380
MAN: Handwriting is so much harder for children with dyspraxia

27
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:44,650
who have problems coordinating movement.

28
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:48,350
MAN: It's good there are lots of things you can do in the classroom to help them.

29
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:51,826
MAN: They need so much more support with letter formation -

30
00:01:51,826 --> 00:01:55,200
you need to play lots of games to help them distinguish letter shapes

31
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:58,430
It takes a lot of patience.

32
00:01:58,430 --> 00:02:02,240
WOMAN: Yeah. I liked the idea of using one of those pens that lights up

33
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,252
if you press too hard.

34
00:02:04,252 --> 00:02:06,290
That seems like a really simple solution.

35
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:13,910
MAN: Yes, absolutely. I'm not sure there's much you can do about children with dyspraxia writing very slowly.

36
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:16,740
MAN: It's more important to focus on accuracy

37
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:21,740
and as they get more confident I think they eventually speed up.

38
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:26,117
WOMAN: One quite simple thing you can do is to use grid paper.

39
00:02:26,117 --> 00:02:28,600
So they write each letter in a box

40
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:31,840
and that trains them to space the letters correctly.

41
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:37,630
MAN: Indeed, that's more important for legibility than trying to get them to write in a straight line.

42
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:46,300
MAN: For some children it might be better to teach them to write on a laptop rather than by hand. Like children with dyslexia.

43
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:50,430
MAN: They often really struggle with handwriting and some just give up.

44
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:55,291
WOMAN: Yeah. It's not as frustrating for them if they get things wrong -

45
00:02:55,291 --> 00:02:58,260
on a keyboard they can be more willing to have a go

46
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:02,930
But I read that developing fluency isn't any faster.

47
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:09,603
MAN: That's right. Did you read that article on the benefits of teaching print rather than cursive handwriting,

48
00:03:09,603 --> 00:03:11,710
where the letters are joined up?

49
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:18,900
WOMAN: Yes. Well in the past cursive writing was certainly considered more stylish and educated.

50
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:22,342
WOMAN: But not anymore. Teachers' attitudes have changed

51
00:03:22,342 --> 00:03:27,506
because it's been proved that cursive is more difficult to learn -

52
00:03:27,506 --> 00:03:29,920
especially for children with learning difficulties

53
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:33,100
who find joining up letters really challenging.

54
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:39,322
MAN: I agree. I was always worried that my poor handwriting affected my exam results.

55
00:03:39,322 --> 00:03:43,470
And now research shows that I was right to worry.

56
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:50,110
MAN: I'm sure a lot of students think it's unfair that they're being judged on their handwriting not just their knowledge.

57
00:03:50,110 --> 00:03:54,394
WOMAN: Marks are definitely affected if examiners can't read the script.

58
00:03:54,394 --> 00:03:59,750
That's why it's always been so important to teach children to write legibly....

59
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:03,250
WOMAN: Do you think the role of handwriting will change in the future?

60
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:06,108
MAN: I can't see that changing much.

61
00:04:06,108 --> 00:04:10,753
Touch typing still isn't taught in most schools, which is a shame.

62
00:04:10,753 --> 00:04:14,326
But maybe that won't be necessary in the future

63
00:04:14,326 --> 00:04:18,970
because people will also be able to write by hand on digital devices.

64
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:25,280
MAN: Anyway, teachers understand the value of handwriting. It's a basic life skill.

65
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:30,724
WOMAN: True. However, the fact is that people are writing by hand less

66
00:04:30,724 --> 00:04:35,750
and less and relying on digital devices. That does cause some problems.

67
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:40,540
MAN: You mean like note taking? There are lots of apps for that.

68
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:43,167
WOMAN: And for reading historical documents apparently.

69
00:04:43,167 --> 00:04:47,340
But my mum is shocked by my awful spelling

70
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:49,922
and the fact that my punctuation is really inconsistent.

71
00:04:49,922 --> 00:04:54,050
I think you can put that down to lack of practice.

72
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:58,500
MAN: I expect so. Personally, I miss writing by hand

73
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:01,360
I hardly ever write anything now.

74
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:07,300
I remember my grandparents had such beautiful handwriting and it was so individual.

75
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:12,430
MAN: Nobody I know would be able to identify my handwriting now. It's a shame.

76
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:14,720
WOMAN: I know. I feel the same way

77
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:20,796
I used to write a diary by hand and now I do that digitally.

78
00:05:20,796 --> 00:05:27,260
It just seems less effort to do it that way. So it's not just a problem...
